Title:
DeepZero — Hunt zero-days at scale (Automated Vulnerability Research Orchestration Engine)

Type:
Documentation (with GitHub Tool)

Short Summary (4–8 sentences max):
DeepZero is a highly concurrent orchestration engine for automated vulnerability research aimed at triaging large binary corpora to surface zero-day candidates. It solves the scaling problem of parsing, decompiling, and analyzing thousands of Windows binaries/drivers by running multi-stage pipelines across available hardware. The workflow resembles a map/reduce pipeline: ingest and parse binaries (e.g., PE parsing, Ghidra headless decompilation), apply parallel heuristic grading (e.g., IOCTL surface filtering, Semgrep rules, known-bad/known-benign hash exclusion), then use LLMs selectively to assess exploitability on high-signal artifacts. It emphasizes resilient, resumable execution with per-sample atomic state to support long-running hunting campaigns. Useful for vulnerability researchers, red teams, and exploit developers who need repeatable large-scale triage rather than manual reverse engineering of every sample. Interesting because it treats LLMs as a late-stage scorer, not the primary analysis engine, reducing noise and cost.

Technical Focus:
- High-concurrency pipeline orchestration (map/reduce-style stages)
- Windows PE and driver corpus parsing at scale
- Headless Ghidra decompilation automation
- Heuristic filtering of Windows IOCTL attack surfaces
- Static analysis rule execution (Semgrep) and hash-based exclusions (loldrivers.io)
- Checkpointing/resumable state for long-running campaigns

Use Cases:
- Large-scale triage of driver corpora (e.g., Snappy Driver Installer) to find exploitable IOCTL handlers
- Automated decompilation + static analysis to prioritize reverse engineering targets
- Filtering out known vulnerable/known noisy samples via hash allow/deny lists (e.g., loldrivers.io)
- Running custom multi-stage pipelines combining heuristics and LLM-based exploitability scoring
- Building repeatable week-long vulnerability hunting campaigns with pause/resume reliability

Keywords:
DeepZero, automated vulnerability research, zero-day hunting, binary triage, Windows drivers, PE parsing, Ghidra headless, decompilation, IOCTL, static analysis, Semgrep, loldrivers.io, corpus analysis, pipeline orchestration, concurrency, map reduce, exploitability assessment, LLM orchestration, resumable workflows, state tracking